    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Step by Step <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Step 1 · One good call</h3>

    <p>
      <strong>Problem:</strong> the same request gives vague, inconsistent answers, and a big model is
      expensive. <strong>Established:</strong> output is controlled by two levers, the
      <strong>prompt</strong> and the <strong>model</strong> (capability vs. cost vs. latency), and a good
      prompt has five parts: Role, Task, Output Format, Examples, Context. A clear decomposition lets a
      cheaper model do the job.
    </p>

    <p>
      Lessons: <a href={L('0001-output-optimization-levers')}>1.1 Levers</a> ·
      <a href={L('0002-persona-role')}>1.2 Persona / Role</a> ·
      <a href={L('0003-prompt-components')}>1.3 Prompt Components</a>
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Step 2 · Make it reason</h3>

    <p>
      <strong>Problem:</strong> the model jumps straight to an answer, and can't use information it
      doesn't have. <strong>Established:</strong> <strong>Chain-of-Thought</strong> makes it write its
      reasoning first: more tokens means more computation, and the written steps act as working memory.
      <strong>ReAct</strong> adds tools: THINK → ACT → OBSERVE, where the <em>model writes the action
      as text</em> and <em>your code runs it</em> and returns real data.
    </p>

    <p>
      Lessons: <a href={L('0004-chain-of-thought-and-react-prompting')}>2.1 CoT &amp; ReAct</a>
      (Demand-Spike Detective) · deep dive: <em>Why "Think Step by Step" Works</em> (LLMs from Scratch)
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Step 3 · Make it precise</h3>

    <p>
      <strong>Problem:</strong> outputs are close, but not what you meant, and not machine-readable.
      <strong>Established:</strong> refinement is an experiment: change one component at a time, compare
      on the same inputs. Missing facts → Context; wrong tone → Role; wrong structure → Output Format.
      "Correct but unparseable" isn't good enough: ask for structure (JSON).
    </p>

    <p>Lesson: <a href={L('0005-prompt-instruction-refinement')}>3.1 Prompt Instruction Refinement</a></p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Step 4 · Make it multi-step</h3>

    <p>
      <strong>Problem:</strong> real tasks have several stages, and one huge prompt misses details.
      <strong>Established:</strong> an <strong>agent</strong> is LLM + tools + instructions + memory +
      orchestration. <strong>Prompt chaining</strong> splits the task into steps, each output feeding the
      next. A fixed chain (<em>workflow</em>) when you know the steps; an agent (ReAct) when the steps
      depend on what's discovered.
    </p>

    <p>
      Lessons: <a href={L('0006-ai-agents-building-blocks')}>4.1 Agent Building Blocks</a> ·
      <a href={L('0007-prompt-chaining')}>4.2 Prompt Chaining</a>
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Step 5 · Make it reliable</h3>

    <p>
      <strong>Problem:</strong> every step can hallucinate or break format, and errors cascade (the
      domino effect). <strong>Established:</strong> put a <strong>contract</strong> on every output (a
      Pydantic model) and a <strong>gate check</strong> after every step: format, content, logic. Use the
      LLM for judgment and plain code for rules. Never pass a failure downstream; every input must end
      up somewhere, even if that's a human.
    </p>

    <p>
      Lessons: <a href={L('0007-prompt-chaining')}>4.2 gate checks</a> ·
      <a href={L('0008-pydantic-structured-data')}>4.3 Pydantic</a> ·
      <a href={L('0009-exercise-claim-triage')}>4.4 Claim Triage</a>
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Step 6 · Make it improve</h3>

    <p>
      <strong>Problem:</strong> a gate can say <em>no</em>, but not make the output better.
      <strong>Established:</strong> <strong>feedback loops</strong> put the evaluation (test results,
      validation errors, self-critique, user input) back into the next prompt and iterate until it
      passes or a limit is hit, with <strong>monitoring</strong> (metrics and a step-by-step trace) to
      see whether the loop converges.
    </p>

    <p>
      Lessons: <a href={L('0010-llm-feedback-loops')}>5.1 LLM Feedback Loops</a> ·
      <a href={L('0012-exercise-code-debugging-assistant')}>5.2 Code Debugging Assistant</a>
    </p>

